# Allowed signers — the trust root for approvals.
#
# Authored, not generated. This file decides whose signature counts as a human approval, and
# `validate_approvals.py` (APV-002) verifies every `approval.commit` against it with
# `git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=<this file> verify-commit`.
#
# ANSWERS — whose signature counts as a human approval.
#
# DOES NOT ANSWER — who may approve what. That is `approval-matrix.md`, and the two are
# deliberately separate: the matrix says who may decide, this file says how we recognise
# them. Someone can be in one and not the other, and both directions are a real finding.
#
# WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN YOUR GLOBAL GIT CONFIG
#
# A trust root a reviewer cannot see in the diff is one an agent could have written. Keeping it
# in version control means adding a signer is a reviewable change with an author and a date,
# and the same repository gives the same answer on every machine. A machine-local
# `gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile` would make the verdict depend on who ran the check.
#
# FILLED IN BADLY WHEN
#
# - An empty file. APV-002 then fails every declared commit, which is correct but unhelpful:
#   it means the project claims witnessed approvals and trusts nobody to give them.
# - A key that belongs to a CI robot or an agent. That key can approve, which converts the
#   whole mechanism back into the thing it replaced. Machine identities sign builds, not
#   governance decisions.
# - A principal that does not match the committer. git matches on the principal, so a typo
#   fails closed — the approval reads as unwitnessed rather than as trusted.
#
# CHECKED BY
#
# APV-002 only. Nothing checks that the people listed here are the people in
# `approval-matrix.md` — that correspondence is APV-003's job, and it compares names rather
# than keys. Someone listed here but not in the matrix can sign a commit git trusts and still
# fail APV-003, which is the intended order: the matrix says who may decide, this file says
# how we recognise them.
#
# FORMAT
#
# One signer per line: <principal> <keytype> <key>
# The principal is normally the signer's email address, matching `user.email` on their commits.
#
#   ada@example.org ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...
#   grace@example.org ssh-rsa AAAAB3NzaC1yc2EAAAADAQAB...
#
# For OpenPGP signatures, leave this file empty and configure a keyring instead; APV-002 will
# then verify against that. The file is still required to exist, so that the decision to rely
# on a keyring is visible here rather than implicit.
#
# AUTHORITY
#
# Adding or removing a line is a governance change under `change-control.md`. It is not an
# edit an agent makes, and an agent that proposes one should say so in a pull request.
